==Latin==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|la|spiciō}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|la|title=speciō|:inh|itc-pro:*spekjō<id:observe>|id=observe}}
{{inh+|la|itc-pro|*spekjō}}, from {{der|la|ine-pro|*spéḱyeti}}.<ref name=EDL>{{R:itc:EDL|pages=578-9|head=speciō, -ere|passage=*spekje/o-}}</ref>

{{lg|cognate|Cognates}} include {{cog|grc|σκέπτομαι}}, {{cog|ae|𐬯𐬞𐬀𐬯𐬫𐬈𐬌𐬙𐬌}}, and {{cog|sa|पश्यति|tr=páśyati}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|speciō}}
* {{hyph|la|spe|ci|ō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|speciō|spex|spect}}

# {{lb|la|OL.|rare}} to [[observe]], [[watch]], [[look at]]
#: {{syn|la|cōnspicor|īnspectō|īnspiciō|intueor|invīsō|spectō|suspiciō|tueor|videō|vīsō}}
#* {{Q|la|Varro|De Lingua Latina|82|quote=''Spectare'' dictum ab antiquo, quo etiam Ennius usus: "Quos Epulo postquam '''spexit'''" . . . et quod in auguriis etiam nunc augures dicunt avem '''specere'''. Consuetudo com<m>unis quae cum praeverbi<nowiki><i></nowiki>s coniun<c>ta fuerunt etiam nunc servat, ut aspicio, conspicio, respicio, suspicio, despicio|t=''Spectare'' derives from an old word, which Ennius also used: "After Epulo '''saw''' them" . . . and because even now, in the taking of auguries, some augurs say '''''specere''''' [to '''watch'''] a bird. Common custom still preserves compounds joined with preverbs, such as ''aspicio'', ''conspicio'', ''respicio'', ''suspicio'', ''despicio''}}
#* {{Q|la|Festus|De Verborum Significatione|446|2|thru=5|quote=''spicit'' quoque sine praepositione dixerunt antiqui . . . et ''spexit''. Ennius lib. XVI: quos ubi rex Epulo spexit de cotibus celsis|t=The ancients also used to say '''''spicit''''' ['''sees'''] without a preposition and '''''spexit''''' ['''saw''']. Ennius. Book 19: "When the king Epulo '''saw''' them from the high rocks"}}

